  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Strong to severe storms expected this afternoon, quiet for \nSaturday, then another chance for severe weather on Sunday. \n\n\nRemnants from an MCS are moving east through Kentucky but continue \nto dissipate. The debris cloud from it is just entering our area, \nand may slightly affect instability, which could affect the coverage \nof afternoon storms. The morning upper-air sounding at Blacksburg \nshowed CAPE values already over 2000 J/kg, with sufficient lapse \nrates and moisture for storm development. The only missing \ningredient is a forcing mechanism, which will come by way of a cold \nfront across the western Ohio Valley that will move towards the area \nlate this afternoon into the evening, along with a shortwave upper-\nlevel trough. Barring any setbacks with instability, scattered to \nwidespread storms are expected to fire mid afternoon along the Blue \nRidge and in the mountains and progress eastward into the Piedmont. \n\nA consistent westerly wind will downslope across the Piedmont today, \nwhich will limit thunderstorm development there and cause some \nstorms to dissipate as the move east off of the Blue Ridge. \nRedevelopment will occur most likely just east of our area. Storms \nthat do survive the trip east will have plenty of energy to work \nwith. Temperatures will be in the upper 90s, and dew points in the \nupper 60s to low 70s. DCAPE values are already over 1000 J/kg, \nindicative of potential downbursts in strong thunderstorms. Storms \nwill have the capability to produce damaging wind gusts and isolated \nlarge hail greater than 1\" in diameter. Shear values are quite low, \nwhich negates any tornado threat. Isolated flash flooding will again \nbe a concern, though storms are expected to move at a steady pace. \nMost likely locations for flash flooding today would be poor \ndrainage areas in urban environments, and the usual flood-prone \nareas. Storms dissipate fully around midnight as the front pushes \noff to the east. \n\nSaturday will be mostly quiet thanks to high pressure moving in from \nthe north, pushing the cold front off to our south briefly, though \nan isolated shower or storm will be possible in Northwest North \nCarolina. The high begins to shift east on Sunday, with another cold \nfront approaching the area in the afternoon, again providing forcing \nfor strong to severe storms, with damaging winds as the main threat. \n\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Above normal temperatures expected today through\nSunday.\n\nTemperatures today look to increase into the upper 90s to near\n100 across the Piedmont, and into the upper 80s to low 90s\nacross mountain locations. Many across the Piedmont are already\nin the low 90s early this afternoon, with several hours of\ndaytime heating still on tap for today. KRNK 18 UTC sounding\nanalyzed an 850mb temperature of 20 Celsius, which will\ncertainly support near 90 degree temperatures at the surface for\nthe NRV, and higher values further east in the Piedmont at \nlower elevations. While a more dry airmass pushes into the \nregion behind the cold front that will pass through the region \ntoday, temperatures on Saturday still look to be hot, with highs \nin the low 90s forecast for the Piedmont, and low to mid 80s for\nmountain locations. The drier airmass will make the air feel \nless muggy on Saturday; however, a return of higher dewpoints on\nSunday combined with increasing high temperatures back into the\nmid 90s for the Piedmont and mid to upper 80s for mountain \nlocations, will increase Heat Index values once again across the\nregion. \n\nWhile Heat Advisory criteria will likely not be met,\ntemperatures will be warm across the region, and sensitive\ngroups should avoid outdoor activity during the peak heating of\nthe day. Those that work outdoors should similarly take frequent\nbreaks indoors with AC, and drink plenty of fluids.",
